WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.700
It's still strange how a story like Stephen
King's It became such a big deal.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.840
But just because something is popular doesn't
mean it's good, much less perfect.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.360
Here's our list of the highlights and lowlights,
and yes, the Deadlights of It: Chapter Two.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.840
Oklahoma's favorite son Bill Hader has really

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.500
been showing off his dramatic chops in recent
years, from his critically-adored turn in

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.420
HBO's complex Barry to his work in It: Chapter
Two as the grown-up Richie Tozier.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.160
In the sequel, the Trashmouth has grown from
a neighborhood nuisance to a national sensation,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.940
or at least something like that as an apparently
well-known stand-up comic.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.860
In his role as the older Richie, Hader builds
on everything that Stranger Things' Finn Wolfhard

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.469
laid down for the character in chapter one,
so much so that it takes no effort to believe

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:48.040
that he's that kid, just 27 years older.

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:52.780
The movie suggests that despite any appearance
of material success, he's not living up to

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.940
his full potential, an intriguing step up
from the radio DJ role he played in King's

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.670
book, with the writing for the character also
being a notable step up from the limp improvised

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.420
jokes Harry Anderson strung together for the
1990 miniseries.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.040
"Let's see my fortune now.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.940
You're gonna be eaten by a big, greasy monster.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.280
Have a nice day!"

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.260
Despite what successes It: Chapter Two may
have in improving on the old miniseries, reviews

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.720
have had one pretty consistent complaint,
the grown-up members of the Losers' Club don't

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.240
really feel like old friends.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.960
They all feel more like they're meeting each
other for the first time, and it's an impression

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.400
that never really goes away.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.130
And before you go pointing out that the group
members being distant towards each other was

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.000
the whole point of the movie, with it all
culminating in the characters reconnecting

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.890
and learning to love one another again, we
would like to counter by saying that at no

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.910
point in this movie does it actually feel
like these people are really connecting with

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:44.480
each other.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.200
Everyone is frigid and chilly towards each
other up until the point that they have to

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.080
wrench the heart out of an ancient cosmic
horror, when they all fight on the same side

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.520
because, well, that's what you're supposed
to do.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.780
One of the biggest strengths of the first
It is the palpable sense of camaraderie that

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.280
can be felt when the young cast is all interacting.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.600
Sparks are flying in these scenes, as are
so, so many f-bombs.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.780
The kid scenes in the sequel still crackle
with nostalgic energy, while the grown-up

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.580
sequences all proceed as though they're dialogues
among strangers killing time at the DMV.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.560
Despite those complaints, don't get us wrong,
It: Chapter Two has assembled a fantastic

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.060
cast.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.120
Seemingly willed into existence by Twitter
posts and fan-casting, the sequel triumphantly

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:29.680
nails a difficult task while making it look
pretty easy.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.909
Casting the same part across generations is
one of those things that you don't really

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:37.030
notice unless it's done in a way that doesn't
really work that well, and the It sequel does

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:42.120
it so seamlessly that it even stops to flex
about it, emphasizing the similarities between

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:47.180
James Ransome and Jack Dylan Grazer as Eddie
Kaspbrak in a show-offy transition shot.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.520
The new cast's appeal isn't all about physical
resemblance.

00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:55.400
James McAvoy deploys an American accent so
convincing that you never question his character's

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:59.700
Maine upbringing, and he also conveys the
perfect level of frustration with his returning

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.640
vocal stutter.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.400
Jay Ryan somehow manages to come off as an
adult Ben Hanscom, one who seems to be aware

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.360
of how hot he's become in adulthood, but knows
he's supposed to be cool about it.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:15.060
Even Andy Bean in his brief turn as the adult
Stan Uris manages to make his few scenes resonate

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.260
by finding the right way to express existential
terror without coming across as too melodramatic.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.840
So even though the cast members rarely seem
like real childhood friends, they do all come

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.689
off as real people in their roles.

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:32.720
Perhaps one reason why the cast has no chemistry
is the fact the movie explicitly demands they

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.260
stop spending time together at a critical
point during their reunion.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.620
After returning to Derry, the group is told
by Mike to split up to recover relics from

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.200
their childhood by themselves.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.660
The middle of the movie follows them all as
they go off on their own specific side missions,

00:03:46.669 --> 00:03:50.499
like it's the end of a video game and we need
one last fetch quest before taking on the

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.480
final boss.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.900
This plotline of needing to gather relics
for the Ritual of Chüd is mostly an invention

00:03:55.919 --> 00:03:59.609
of the movies, which stretches to come up
with a good reason for the characters to spend

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:00.300
time apart.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.280
As a result, we end up spending way too much
of the movie watching the characters be isolated,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.010
facing their fears in an assembly line fashion
that gets dull fast.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.909
Logically, the characters would probably want
to stick together after realizing what Mike's

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:17.069
told them is all real, and they're fighting
a vengeful demon whom they only beat the first

00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:18.400
time by sticking together.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:22.919
Instead, the movie invents a reason for them
to arbitrarily split up, not really because

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.440
the story demands it, but more because the
screenplay's structure does.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.600
One of the reasons why the cast's lack of
chemistry is so frustrating has to do with

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:36.710
the one scene with them all together that
the movie 100% nails; the initial reunion

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.680
sequence at the Jade of the Orient Chinese
restaurant.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.360
In King's book, this scene is less of a fun
hangout sequence and more of an exposition

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.840
engine, laying track for the plot to come,
and the 1990 miniseries version of the scene

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.100
is just weird, feeling very awkward and made-for-TV.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.430
But It: Chapter Two brings the scene to life
with an electric energy, with the memories

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.950
of the Losers' Club seeming to come back in
real time as the protagonists loosen up, both

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:04.760
through their renewed proximity to each other
and the curative powers of alcohol.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:09.180
The only place where this scene doesn't improve
on the old miniseries or the book is the climactic

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.120
invasion of It as a bowl of demented fortune
cookies, which jump up off the table and turn

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.280
into CGI monstrosities that kind of all look
like the horror movie equivalent of the Sonic

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:19.780
trailer.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:23.980
"Meow?"

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.020
One of the most consistent sins of It: Chapter
Two, aside from its over-reliance on jump

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.380
scares and loud noises, is the overuse of
way too much not-quite-there CGI during the

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.840
movie's horror sequences.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.040
It almost feels like the movie overloads on
creepy music and startling, monster-leaps-at-the-screen

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.260
moments to make up for the fact that, on its
own, a lot of this CGI stuff just isn't scary.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.040
CGI creature effects work best in something
like a lighthearted Avengers-style movie,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.599
when you want to buy into the comic book fiction
of it all and let the movie get away with

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.600
things that look less than realistic.

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:03.060
But that's generally not how things work with
horror, which gets its most effective scares

00:06:03.069 --> 00:06:07.479
out of keeping the viewer engaged and drawing
them into a nightmarish moment, making them

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.229
believe, if even for just a second, that everything
they're seeing is real.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.479
When a supposedly horrific sight makes you
think more about what's new on Xbox than the

00:06:15.479 --> 00:06:19.681
fact that you're gonna die one day, it makes
the scares much less effective, and the movie

00:06:19.681 --> 00:06:22.480
they're in less capable of standing the test
of time.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.480
That said, don't get us wrong.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.180
There are parts of It: Chapter Two in which
the horror all really works.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.380
Critics have said that Chapter Two is less
scary than its 2017 predecessor, and while

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:37.850
this may be true by the time the Losers' Club
is fighting Pennywise as a giant spider and

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:41.900
defeating it by screaming the f-word, it's
not really true for the entire movie.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.539
Indeed, some sequences in the sequel shiver
with such horror that they trounce anything

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.520
the first movie put forward as a scare.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.740
Despite all the child slayings, the first
It movie had a tendency to feel more like

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:56.320
a thrill ride than anything close to being
psychologically affecting.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.120
That's not the case for some of It: Chapter
Two's most genuinely disturbing scenes, which

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.270
include the vicious assault and killing of
a young gay man at the beginning of the movie,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:10.260
to Pennywise sickly manipulating and devouring
a little girl underneath some Derry bleachers.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.020
Real horror should do more than just startle
you, it should make you feel nasty, and stick

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.780
to you like a greasy slime.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.469
Pennywise leaping out of the dark with his
claws out might make you jump while you're

00:07:20.469 --> 00:07:24.600
watching the movie, but these are the sequences
that stick with you in the nights that follow.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:29.480
Of course, there's a lot to remember about
It: Chapter Two once you're done watching

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.699
it, mostly because this sequel is a lot of
movie.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.300
Like, too much movie.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:40.160
At 165 minutes, the sequel's runtime edges
up toward Avengers: Endgame levels, but that

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.160
movie was MCU Chapter Twenty-Two, not Oh-Two.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.080
"It's, uh, a long story."

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:49.659
While the movie doesn't feel truly punishing
in its length on a first watch, it does feel

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:53.889
every minute of its long length, and it doesn't
really feel like an experience that digs deep

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:57.960
enough into its themes or characters to really
warrant that massive runtime.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.510
So much of the movie is given over to catching
the audience up on where the characters are

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:06.660
at in their lives, and then giving them each
a protracted solo scare sequence, a structure

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.530
that gets repetitive and tiresome well before
the final battle comes around.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.800
You can say a lot of things about Stephen

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:18.270
King, but one thing you can't deny is that
the dude has a big imagination.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:26.090
Potent, eerie, too-hot-for-TV imagination,
to the tune of 1,138 pages of often insane

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:26.620
storytelling.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:31.600
As a book, It is full of rich details and
left-turn sequences that seem inspired, at

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.720
least in part, by a lenient editor and a lot
of cocaine.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.920
"For me, the fun of writing novels isn't the
finished product, which I don't care about

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:42.420
that much."

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.040
It's to the credit of It: Chapter Two that
the sequel incorporates so many of the novel's

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.900
weirdest elements, making the already strange
story of a shapeshifting killer clown even

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.360
weirder this time around.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.300
Though the two It movies leave plenty of the
more bizarre elements of King's story out

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:01.779
of the adaptation, it's admirable just how
much corny crap the movie commits to getting

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:05.769
into, especially when it doesn't really need
to explain too much about what's going on

00:09:05.769 --> 00:09:06.760
in Derry at all.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.340
Once the movie starts running through weird
things like the Ritual of Chüd and Its true

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:15.380
form as the cosmic Deadlights, you know you're
watching a movie with some goofball genre

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:15.880
swagger.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:20.820
This is a story that trusts you to stay with
it through every weird turn, up to and including

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.740
the part where the Losers kill Pennywise by
basically just hurting its feelings.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:30.220
Though It: Chapter Two is willing to go to
some strange places when it comes to Its ancient

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.700
cosmic origins, the movie doesn't exactly
go whole hog with everything the book has

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:35.300
to offer.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.940
And we're not just talking about the novel's
space turtle.

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.160
No, there are plenty of other story aspects
from King's thick novel that just don't get

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.899
a lot of play here, from the way the mere
presence of It is implied to bring out the

00:09:45.899 --> 00:09:50.470
evil in Derry's residents, to the wider lives
of the grown-up Losers, whose loved ones play

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:54.360
more into the resolution of the story in the
book than the movie wants to get into.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.860
One other notable loss is the instantly-abandoned
storyline of the grown-up Bev's abusive husband,

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.900
who is given one scene to be a bad guy before
Bev flees.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:07.600
This entire aspect of her life and character
remains totally unresolved, and it speaks

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.320
to a general lack of thematic storytelling
in It's second installment.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:16.779
If the first movie was about childhood and
loss of innocence, the second movie is about...what,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:17.320
exactly?

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:21.540
For all its length and bluster, It: Chapter
Two isn't really about anything other than

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.920
killing that clown.

